  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\n\nNorthern part of a strong mid level ridge centered over the \nsoutheast U.S. is suppressed as westerlies drop south thru the Great \nLakes into the Ohio Valley. This will allow a slow moving cold front \nto drop thru ILN/s northwest counties this morning and the remainder \nof the area this afternoon. This front is an ana front with showers \nalong and behind the frontal boundary. Instability is very marginal \nwith forecast soundings showing a very skinny cape profile. As the \nfront slips into the area this morning expect mainly showers with a \nfew embedded thunderstorms possible over the south this afternoon. \n\nClouds, rain, and northwest to north winds at 10-15 mph in the post \nfrontal environment will make for a cool day. Expect little diurnal \nrise with temperatures in the 60s after the frontal passage. \n\n\nKey Message 2)\n\nIn the wake of today's front surface high pressure will build across\nthe Great Lakes this weekend. This will offer dry conditions and\ntemperatures around 5 degrees below normal Saturday. A brief\nmoderation to near normal temperatures on Sunday will be followed by\ncooler air as Canadian high pressure builds southeast into the \nregion early next week. High temperatures will be mainly in the 60s \nMonday and Tuesday - around 7 to 10 degrees below normal. Lows look \nto be around 10 degrees below normal with the potential for patchy \nfrost Monday night and Tuesday night. Readings moderate to near or \nslightly above normal for the end of the week.",
